---
import DocLayout from '../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import BindingLinks from '../../components/BindingLinks.astro';
import BindingMatrix from '../../components/BindingMatrix.astro';
import {
  bindings,
  contributingHref,
  demandDrivenBindings,
  plannedBindings,
} from '../../data/bindings';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/paths';
---

<DocLayout
  title="Language integrations"
  description="One Rust engine. Explicit capability, qualification, and distribution boundaries."
  back="/"
  backLabel="Cribra"
>
  <p>
    Native and WebAssembly adapters project Cribra Core semantics. They preserve classification
    authority, review-only candidates, UTF-8 coordinates, and explicit transformations without
    creating another detection engine.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>TypeScript:</strong> the WebAssembly package is implemented with the approved identity
    <code>@metalymph/cribra@0.1.0</code> but is unpublished. It is not yet an available registry
    installation instruction, and no npm registry record exists. Start with the
    <a href={withBase('/docs/typescript/')}>TypeScript quick start</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>React Native:</strong> the native package
    <code>@metalymph/cribra-react-native@0.0.0</code> is implemented and qualified, but it is private
    and unpublished. Install it from a local checkout with a <code>file:</code> dependency. Start
    with the <a href={withBase('/docs/integration-react-native/')}>React Native integration guide</a>.
    It is a separate native TurboModule over the C ABI, not a mode of the WebAssembly package.
  </p>
  <h2>Start with an integration guide</h2>
  <p>
    Every implemented integration has a directly accessible guide. Each card also links the
    integration's existing API reference, package, or discovery listing where one exists.
  </p>
  <ul class="binding-guide-list">
    {
      bindings.map((binding) => (
        <li>
          <h3>{binding.name}</h3>
          <BindingLinks binding={binding} />
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <BindingMatrix />
  <h2>Choose a matching profile</h2>
  <p>
    For UniFFI consumers, use one matching generated binding, native library, and profile set per
    process. Kotlin requires one profile JAR per classpath; Swift requires one package exposing <code
      >Cribra</code
    >. Streaming admission and resource limits remain Core-owned.
  </p>
  <h2>API references</h2>
  <p>
    The generated <a href={withBase('/api/0.7.2/swift/core/documentation/cribra/')}
      >Swift Core API reference</a
    > is published for Cribra 0.7.2. It covers the Swift <code>Cribra</code> module for the
    <strong>Core</strong> profile.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href={withBase('/docs/typescript-api-reference/')}>TypeScript API reference</a> documents
    the eight public runtime exports, <code>Scanner</code>, options, DTOs, UTF-8 coordinates, errors,
    ownership and disposal for the approved, unpublished WebAssembly package.
  </p>
  <p>
    Flow and Fuse are capability profiles. Fuse is the published official remote package for Swift;
    Core and Flow remain source-build alternatives. Core reference availability does not imply
    complete API coverage for Flow or Fuse, and no Flow or Fuse DocC reference is published. No
    other generated binding has a published DocC reference on this site.
  </p>
  <p>
    See <a href={withBase('/docs/integration-swift/')}>Swift integration</a> for Fuse installation
    and the documented Core/Flow source-build paths. The Fuse package is also listed on the <a
      href="https://swiftpackageindex.com/Metalymph/cribra-swift">Swift Package Index</a
    > for discovery; the listing is not Cribra compatibility-build evidence.
  </p>
  <h2>Official planned integrations</h2>
  <p>
    The first adoption priority is completing the official
    <code>@metalymph/cribra@0.1.0</code> npm release; the package is prepared and qualified but not
    published. The integrations below follow in order. Except where the matrix above records
    otherwise, they are not implemented, qualified or published. The TypeScript WebAssembly package
    is implemented and qualified from installed tarballs, but remains unpublished and is therefore
    not listed here as planned. The React Native package is likewise implemented and qualified but
    private and unpublished, so it is listed above rather than here.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      plannedBindings.map((binding) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{binding.name}</strong> — {binding.detail}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <h2>Demand-driven and community candidates</h2>
  <p>
    C#, Go and PHP are planned as demand-driven / community-contribution candidates. They are not
    actively scheduled, and no package or integration is available. Ruby, Elixir and other
    candidates retain the same status. Doxygen-generated C API documentation and GitHub/GitLab
    integrations are likewise explicitly demand-driven and unscheduled. The project will consider
    them only with demonstrated real-world demand or a viable community contribution, and no
    delivery dates are promised.
  </p>
  <ul>{demandDrivenBindings.map((name) => <li>{name}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>
    Community contributions are welcome under the <a href={contributingHref}
      >contribution guidelines</a
    >. Official support requires an ongoing maintenance commitment, accurate documentation,
    consumer testing and CI coverage. Using a public interoperability surface does not by itself
    make a binding official.
  </p>
  <p>
    See the <a
      href="https://github.com/Metalymph/cribra/blob/main/docs/ECOSYSTEM.md#binding-support-policy"
      >binding support policy</a
    >, <a href="https://github.com/Metalymph/cribra/blob/main/ROADMAP.md"
      >roadmap</a
    > and <a href="https://github.com/Metalymph/cribra/blob/main/docs/INTEROP.md"
      >interoperability contract</a
    > for responsibilities and future qualification gates.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
